// The three CPU-PARALLEL QUERIES a program reaches through `__Builtins.cpuCount()`,
// `__Builtins.schedMaxActiveWorkers()` and `__Builtins.schedProcessorCount()`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THEY ARE THREE QUESTIONS IN TWO BANDS, AND THE SPLIT IS WHAT DECIDES WHICH TARGETS MAY RUN THEM.**
// One asks the MACHINE how parallel a run COULD be; the other two ask THIS COMPILER'S SCHEDULER how parallel
// it was CONFIGURED to be and how parallel it actually GOT.
//
//   • `__cpu_count` is an OS read, so it lowers only where a lane provides `HostFacility.cpuCount` and is
//     refused at the call's own span everywhere else (E3104, keyed on the `__cpu_` band in
//     `TargetFacilities.calleeHostFacility`).
//   • `__sched_max_active_workers` and `__sched_processor_count` reach no OS at all: each is one `.data`
//     load, which lowers on every target the compiler emits. Refusing either would be refusing a load.
//
// ⭐ **WHY THE SCHEDULER PAIR IS NOT `runtime/`'s GREEN-THREAD VOCABULARY.** A program may legitimately ask
// how parallel it got having spawned nothing, so neither query may drag the scheduler in behind it. Each
// rides its own `RuntimeUsage` bit, and that bit is what lays the word out
// (`SchedRuntime.schedRuntimeGlobals`) — so the question costs one word and never a scheduler.
//
// ⚠ **NEITHER WORD IS SEEDED THE SAME WAY, AND THE ASYMMETRY IS THE TRUTH ABOUT AN UNINITIALIZED PROCESS.**
// The worker mark is seeded to 1 because a program with no scheduler really does run on one M — its own;
// the processor count is seeded to 0 because a scheduler that has not initialized has built no P.
//
// ⚠ **THE WORKER MARK IS A MEASUREMENT AND THE PROCESSOR COUNT IS A RESOLUTION**, which is why a case about
// `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` must assert the second. `__sched_worker_loop` raises the mark when a worker M enters, so
// a program that finishes its backlog before a second M is ever needed reads 1 at four processors —
// legitimately. `emitResolveMaxProcs` settles `__sched_num_procs` before any green thread runs and without
// consulting the workload, so two runs of one program on one machine cannot disagree about it.

// ⭐ **FULL-RANGE SIGNED, AND THE HOST'S TWO FAILURE ANSWERS ARE WHY.** `GetActiveProcessorCount` reports 0
// and `sysconf` reports -1, so the floor test below must read SIGNED — `MachineWord` is a `bits(64)` pattern
// and every comparison on one reads unsigned, which turns -1 into the largest count the machine could have.
// It cannot be narrowed to the post-clamp `1 to i64.max` either: a narrowed alias is a guarded door, and the
// guard would panic on a word the host itself wrote, in a body with no caller to report to.
//
// ⚠ **THE NAME SAYS *REPORTED* BECAUSE `ProcessorCount` IS TAKEN BY A TYPE THAT CANNOT HOLD WHAT THIS ONE
// DOES.** `Testing/SpecParser.maxon` publishes `ProcessorCount = int(1 to i64.max)` for an author-written
// `<!-- procs: N -->`, where the decimal parse guarantees the floor. This alias is the HOST's answer before
// any floor exists, and 0 and -1 are the two readings it is declared to carry. Sharing the name would assert
// the two ranges agree, and they deliberately do not — `runtime/Clock.maxon`'s `EpochSeconds` is the same
// ruling one family over.
typealias ReportedProcessorCount = int(i64.min to i64.max)

// The floor a processor count never answers below. A count is a divisor for callers, so a 0 must never
// escape.
//
// ⚠ **`SchedRuntime` CLAMPS THE SAME OP AGAINST THE SAME FLOOR AND SPELLS THE NUMBER AGAIN**, because no
// name crosses the tier boundary — the restatement `runtime/Clock.maxon` makes of the FILETIME constants,
// for the same reason.
let MinimumProcessorCount = 1 as ReportedProcessorCount

// ⭐⭐ **`__cpu_count()` — HOW MANY LOGICAL PROCESSORS THE HOST REPORTS, NEVER BELOW 1.**
//
// ⚠ **THE FLOOR IS NOT DEAD CODE.** `GetActiveProcessorCount` answers 0 for an invalid processor group and
// `sysconf` answers -1, so the arm is what a failing host read reaches. One signed test covers both
// spellings, which is why a lane supplies only the read.
module function __cpu_count() returns ReportedProcessorCount
	let reported = __Raw.osCpuCount() as ReportedProcessorCount

	if reported < MinimumProcessorCount 'belowFloor'
		return MinimumProcessorCount
	end 'belowFloor'

	return reported
end '__cpu_count'

// ⭐ **`__sched_max_active_workers()` — THE HIGH-WATER MARK OF CONCURRENTLY-ACTIVE WORKER Ms.**
//
// ⚠ **THE IOCP COMPLETION THREAD IS NOT A WORKER M.** `__io_init` creates an OS thread, but it drains
// completions and re-readies parked green threads — it never RUNS one and never adopts a P. The mark is
// raised in `__sched_worker_loop` and nowhere else.
//
// ⚠ **IT ANSWERS 1 FOR AN `async` PROGRAM AT ANY PROCESSOR COUNT.** An `async` frame is a coroutine of its
// caller and a member of its strand, and a member readied while its strand is held owes no wake, so an
// `async` program that reads no pipe creates no worker M. A `spawn` does, and raises the mark.
module function __sched_max_active_workers() returns MachineWord
	return __Raw.loadWord(__Raw.schedMaxActiveWorkersAddr(), offset: 0)
end '__sched_max_active_workers'

// ⭐ **`__sched_processor_count()` — HOW MANY Ps `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` RESOLVED TO**, which is the one thing that
// variable actually decides.
//
// ⚠ The word is `__sched_num_procs`, so the ADDRESS ROW and the entry point do not share a name. The word is
// the P array's length wearing this query's second hat (`SchedRuntime.SchedNumProcsLabel`), and this body
// must read that word rather than a second one kept in step with it.
module function __sched_processor_count() returns MachineWord
	return __Raw.loadWord(__Raw.schedNumProcsAddr(), offset: 0)
end '__sched_processor_count'
